{
  "portfolio_size": 10,
  "conflicts": [
    {
      "company": "ServiceTrack",
      "type": "direct",
      "severity": "blocking",
      "rationale": "ServiceTrack operates in Field Service Management \u2014 the same sector and core use case as Cadence (scheduling, dispatch, job management for SMB service businesses). Investing in Cadence would create a direct intra-portfolio conflict."
    },
    {
      "company": "CrewOps",
      "type": "direct",
      "severity": "blocking",
      "rationale": "CrewOps focuses on workforce scheduling, which is a primary feature of Cadence's platform (crew and job scheduling for SMB field service businesses). The core product functionality directly overlaps."
    },
    {
      "company": "JobberPlus",
      "type": "direct",
      "severity": "blocking",
      "rationale": "JobberPlus is an SMB Operations SaaS targeting the same customer segment as Cadence. Both offer all-in-one job, crew, and customer management for SMB service businesses, making this a direct competitive conflict."
    },
    {
      "company": "PatchWork",
      "type": "adjacent",
      "severity": "manageable",
      "rationale": "PatchWork is a home services marketplace, which overlaps with a key vertical Cadence serves (home service SMBs). While PatchWork is a marketplace and Cadence is SaaS tooling, they share the same customer base and could compete for SMB operator relationships."
    },
    {
      "company": "FieldForce",
      "type": "direct",
      "severity": "manageable",
      "rationale": "FieldForce operated in Field Service Management \u2014 the same sector as Cadence \u2014 but has exited the portfolio, making the conflict historical rather than active. Severity is manageable given the exit."
    },
    {
      "company": "QuoteWave",
      "type": "adjacent",
      "severity": "manageable",
      "rationale": "QuoteWave provides SMB quoting and invoicing, which is an adjacent workflow to Cadence's job and customer management platform. Both target SMB service businesses and natural product expansion by either company could create direct competition."
    },
    {
      "company": "InspectAI",
      "type": "adjacent",
      "severity": "manageable",
      "rationale": "InspectAI serves property inspection, a field service vertical that Cadence's scheduling and dispatch platform could expand into. Shared vertical and potential customer overlap among field service SMBs."
    },
    {
      "company": "RouteIQ",
      "type": "adjacent",
      "severity": "manageable",
      "rationale": "RouteIQ handles last-mile delivery logistics including routing and dispatch, which overlaps with Cadence's dispatch and job scheduling capabilities. Both serve businesses with field-based crews and share technical adjacency in route optimization."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "total_checked": 10,
    "conflict_count": 8,
    "has_blocking_conflict": true,
    "overall_severity": "blocking"
  },
  "validation": {
    "status": "valid",
    "errors": []
  },
  "metadata": {
    "run_id": "20260622T190427Z"
  }
}

{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "assessment_mode_intentional": true,
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "The company has genuine category-creation potential — right macro timing, strong early PMF signal, and a team whose builder backgrounds map unusually well to PLG distribution for SMB field service. In any other portfolio context I would be fighting for this deal. But with ServiceTrack, CrewOps, and JobberPlus all active and in direct overlap, the conflict is categorical and non-negotiable. The vision is fundable — just not by us."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "The execution signals are real: sub-4-month CAC payback, 18% MoM growth sustained over four months, a complementary founding team. But the portfolio conflict with three active investments in direct overlap is a structural dealbreaker that no level of traction can override. Beyond the conflict, I would still need to see the GTM playbook, retention data, and churn before I had full conviction — but those are solvable gaps at the right fund."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "hard_pass",
      "rationale": "The unit economics are exceptional for seed stage: ~4.5-month CAC payback, 80% gross margins, directional LTV/CAC of ~11x. The $16M pre is defensible at 18% MoM growth, though at the top of the range. The portfolio conflict across three holdings is an unambiguous dealbreaker under our evaluation criteria. Even setting aside the conflict, the absence of cohort retention data, burn rate, and customer concentration figures leaves too many critical unknowns for full conviction."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
    {
      "topic": "Opening: What Do We Actually Have Here?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I want to start by saying — I pulled up Cadence's numbers before this meeting and this is one of the better seed deals we've seen this quarter. $40K MRR, 18% month-over-month for four straight months, 120 paying customers. That's not manufactured. And the team — ex-Stripe infrastructure, ex-Notion PM — that's the exact combination you want for a PLG-first go-to-market into SMBs. The FSM incumbents have all moved upmarket. ServiceTitan is chasing enterprise. Jobber is plateauing. There is a real white space here for a modern, product-led entrant targeting the 1-to-10-technician segment."
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I agree the traction is real. The CAC-to-payback math is actually exceptional for SMB SaaS — under four months is not something you see often. And I like that these founders are clearly shipping fast. The Notion PM background tells me the product is probably genuinely usable for a field service owner-operator who doesn't want to spend three days in an onboarding session. But I have to stop you before we go further — we have ServiceTrack and CrewOps and JobberPlus in the portfolio. All three are active. All three are in direct overlap. How do we even have this meeting?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "I ran the numbers before this meeting too. The gross margins are best-in-class — 80% is top-tier SaaS. The implied LTV/CAC at even conservative churn assumptions is north of 10x. The valuation — $16M pre on $480K ARR — is about 33x, which is elevated but not outrageous for 18% MoM at seed. In any other context I would be asking for the data room. But I flagged the conflict issue immediately when this crossed my desk. We have three blocking conflicts. That's not a gray area."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "The Portfolio Conflict: Is There Any Path Through?",
      "exchanges": [
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "Let me steelman the counterargument for a moment, because I think we owe it to ourselves to think this through. ServiceTrack is a different product segment — they're mid-market, not true SMB. CrewOps is workforce scheduling, which has meaningful overlap but isn't identical to field service dispatch. And JobberPlus — honestly, I'm not sure they're growing anymore. Are we sure the conflict is as clean as it looks on paper?"
        },
        {
          "partner": "operator",
          "position": "I hear you trying to find a path, but I've been to the ServiceTrack and JobberPlus board meetings. Both of those companies are actively selling to the 1-to-20-technician segment. If Cadence closes a customer who was evaluating JobberPlus, we're going to hear about it. And beyond the business reality, the legal question is what keeps me up at night. Our term sheets with all three companies have information-sharing provisions and in some cases board observer rights. If we're getting Cadence's financials and growth data while sitting on the cap tables of their competitors, we have a problem. Legal will not clear this."
        },
        {
          "partner": "analyst",
          "position": "The steelman doesn't hold up quantitatively. All three companies target the same buyer profile — service SMBs with field crews. The customer overlap test is not about product feature sets; it's about whether the same buyer is evaluating both. And at $333 average ARPU, Cadence is targeting the same cash-strapped owner-operator that ServiceTrack and JobberPlus are both selling to. There is no meaningful segmentation argument here. The conflict is blocking and it is real."
        },
        {
          "partner": "visionary",
          "position": "I concede the point. I've been trying to find a way to invest in this company because I think it's genuinely good. But I can't construct an argument that gets us past the fiduciary reality. If we invest in Cadence and one of our existing portfolio companies loses a deal to them, or finds out we shared their board-level data with a competitor, we lose LP trust that took years to build. It's not worth it. I'm with you both — hard pass."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Quality of the Business: What Would We Tell a Friendly Fund?",
      "exchanges": [
